<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Muwaffaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/842">842</a>–<a href="/years/891">891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Abu Ahmad Talha ibn al-Mutawakkil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Muwaffaq was an Abbasid prince, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mutawakkil, who held real power during the reign of his brother al-Mu'tamid from the 870s until his death in 891. He stopped the Saffarid Ya'qub ibn al-Layth at Dayr al-Aqul in 876 and led the long campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a>, storming their capital al-Mukhtara in 883. His son became caliph as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Talha, known as Abu Ahmad, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mutawakkil and a Greek concubine, Ashar, called Umm Ishaq. According to the historian al-Tabari, he was drinking with his father on the night in 861 when Turkish soldiers murdered him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, and withdrew after a brief attempt to defend him. Hugh Kennedy suggests that his close later ties with the Turkish commanders may have been formed then or before. During the turmoil known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a> he led the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> against Caliph al-Musta'in from February to December 865, but his brother al-Mu'tazz later imprisoned him as a rival. In 869 he declined the caliphate when the army and people called for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 870 he and the Turkish commander Musa ibn Bugha took control of the government, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'tamid little more than his title. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in 871 he received a vast governorship and the name al-Muwaffaq Billah, Blessed of God, and in 875 he was placed in the succession after al-Mu'tamid's son. With his secretaries, among them Sa'id ibn Makhlad, he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and from 877 appointed the caliph's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> himself.</p><p>When the Saffarid Ya'qub ibn al-Layth advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, he and Musa routed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dayr-al-aqul">Battle of Dayr al-Aqul</a>, saving Baghdad. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a>, whose slave rebellion in the marshes of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> had begun in 869, he joined his son Abu'l-Abbas in 880 and took their capital, Mukhtara, in August 883, earning the title al-Nasir li-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>. He failed to unseat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and in 882 had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> seized on his flight towards Ibn Tulun and held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>; in 886 he recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> as hereditary governors of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for 30 years. Campaigns against Amr ibn al-Layth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> won ground in 886 and 887, but settlements later restored Amr's position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 889 he had Abu'l-Abbas imprisoned, for reasons that are unclear. Crippled by gout, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in May 891 from two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, and the troops' support for his son forced his release and recognition as heir. Al-Muwaffaq died on 2 June 891 and was buried in al-Rusafah near his mother. His defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> began a recovery of caliphal power that culminated when Abu'l-Abbas became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> in 892.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Muwaffaq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Abbasid regent <b>al-Muwaffaq</b> and his son, the future caliph al-Mu'tadid, built a fleet and siege city opposite al-Mukhtara and stormed it in 883; Ali ibn Muhammad was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/zanj-state">Zanj state</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.2,43.89&amp;year=873" aria-label="Samarra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5667 925 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Muwaffaq"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6117 1195h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6117 1195h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.2,43.89&amp;year=873">What was here in 873 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
